{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3cf","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a071e4-03f6-7fa0-b2ec-dd2af9ddca4b"},"sequence":66,"recorded_at":1780531400000127,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"ade69f2b-bbb2-4158-ab5d-a5e12db94d0c","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"ade69f2b-bbb2-4158-ab5d-a5e12db94d0c","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"ade69f2b-bbb2-4158-ab5d-a5e12db94d0c"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"c537efb5-e813-4c10-bd26-329a56bdc20c"},"task_id":"c537efb5-e813-4c10-bd26-329a56bdc20c","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"c537efb5-e813-4c10-bd26-329a56bdc20c","chunk":"Read text file `internal/provhost/surrogate.go`.\n1|package provhost\n2|\n3|// This file is the local lone-surrogate gate for the Section 1.6 wire\n4|// bodies this package decodes. Pinned specification Section 1.6 requires\n5|// decoders to reject lone surrogate code points before canonicalization,\n6|// and the repository already enforces that rule in\n7|// internal/canonicaljson (validateSurrogateEscapes, called from\n8|// decodeStrict). This package decodes with its own strict object reader\n9|// (decodeStrictObject in protocol.go) because it must keep member-level\n10|// fault attribution (duplicate member names, the offending member) that\n11|// the shared decoder does not report, and encoding/json silently replaces\n12|// a lone \\ud800-style escape with U+FFFD instead of failing. So the gate\n13|// lives here, scans the raw frame bytes before decoding, and refuses the\n14|// same class the shared package refuses.\n15|//\n16|// The gate is deliberately a copy, not an import: internal/canonicaljson\n17|// is byte-identical shared baseline text that two unlanded stories must\n18|// not edit at once, so neither story may add an export there. The\n19|// surrogate-vector sweep in surrogate_test.go drives both this gate\n20|// (through decodeStrictObject) and canonicaljson.Canonicalize over a\n21|// derived enumeration of the code-point space, so the copies cannot\n22|// drift silently: a divergence reddens there rather than passing\n23|// unnoticed. The sweep judges verdicts against the independent\n24|// implementation, never against a shared hand-written expectation.\n25|//\n26|// The gate decides the surrogate-escape question only. Malformed\n27|// escapes and unterminated strings are not its verdict: they fall\n28|// through to the JSON-syntax arms that already own them. Non-UTF-8\n29|// bytes never reach it: decodeStrictObject refuses them first,\n30|// because a raw WTF-8 surrogate encoding would otherwise slip past\n31|// this escape scanner and be silently replaced with U+FFFD by\n32|// encoding/json. A lone high surrogate is a high unit not immediately\n33|// followed by a \\uDC00–\\uDFFF escape; a lone low surrogate is a low\n34|// unit in any other position. Both are refused before any member is\n35|// read.\n36|\n37|// hasLoneSurrogateEscape reports whether raw JSON bytes carry a lone\n38|// surrogate escape inside any string. The string walk mirrors the shared\n39|// package's walker (backslash handling included: a \\\\ escape consumes\n40|// both bytes, so the text \"\\\\ud800\" is backslash-plus-text, not an\n41|// escape), but only the surrogate verdict is returned.\n42|func hasLoneSurrogateEscape(input []byte) bool {\n43|\tfor index := 0; index < len(input); index++ {\n44|\t\tif input[index] != '\"' {\n45|\t\t\tcontinue\n46|\t\t}\n47|\t\tfor index++; index < len(input); index++ {\n48|\t\t\tif input[index] == '\"' {\n49|\t\t\t\tgoto stringComplete\n50|\t\t\t}\n51|\t\t\tif input[index] != '\\\\' {\n52|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n53|\t\t\t}\n54|\t\t\tindex++\n55|\t\t\tif index >= len(input) || input[index] != 'u' {\n56|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n57|\t\t\t}\n58|\t\t\tunit, end, ok := readHexUnit(input, index-1)\n59|\t\t\tif !ok {\n60|\t\t\t\t// Not a well-formed \\uXXXX escape: the\n61|\t\t\t\t// JSON-syntax arms own it, not this gate.\n62|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n63|\t\t\t}\n64|\t\t\tindex = end - 1\n65|\t\t\tif unit >= 0xd800 && unit <= 0xdbff {\n66|\t\t\t\tlow, lowEnd, ok := readHexUnit(input, end)\n67|\t\t\t\tif !ok || low < 0xdc00 || low > 0xdfff {\n68|\t\t\t\t\treturn true\n69|\t\t\t\t}\n70|\t\t\t\tindex = lowEnd - 1\n71|\t\t\t} else if unit >= 0xdc00 && unit <= 0xdfff {\n72|\t\t\t\treturn true\n73|\t\t\t}\n74|\t\t}\n75|\t\t// The string never closed: malformed JSON the syntax arms\n76|\t\t// refuse, so the gate claims no surrogate verdict here.\n77|\t\treturn false\n78|\tstringComplete:\n79|\t}\n80|\treturn false\n81|}\n82|\n83|// readHexUnit parses one \\uXXXX escape at input[start]. It reports false\n84|// when the bytes are not a well-formed escape; hex digits parse in\n85|// either case, matching the shared package's ParseUint base-16 read.\n86|func readHexUnit(input []byte, start int) (uint16, int, bool) {\n87|\tif start < 0 || start+6 > len(input) || input[start] != '\\\\' || input[start+1] != 'u' {\n88|\t\treturn 0, start, false\n89|\t}\n90|\tvar value uint16\n91|\tfor _, b := range input[start+2 : start+6] {\n92|\t\tvar digit byte\n93|\t\tif b >= '0' && b <= '9' {\n94|\t\t\tdigit = b - '0'\n95|\t\t} else if b >= 'a' && b <= 'f' {\n96|\t\t\tdigit = b - 'a' + 10\n97|\t\t} else if b >= 'A' && b <= 'F' {\n98|\t\t\tdigit = b - 'A' + 10\n99|\t\t} else {\n100|\t\t\treturn 0, start, false\n101|\t\t}\n102|\t\tvalue = value*16 + uint16(digit)\n103|\t}\n104|\treturn value, start + 6, true\n105|}"}}}
